  "evidence_gaps": [
    "No direct absolute oral bioavailability measurement for the query molecule is present in the supplied records.",
    "No matched oral-versus-IV exposure data, dose, formulation, or fed/fasted condition is available for the query.",
    "No measured permeability, solubility, or transporter data for the query are supplied; the low prior is physicochemical only.",
    "The closest analog, azosemide, has F ≈ 20.4%, which is close to the threshold, so the prediction is sensitive to formulation and condition differences.",
    "Food effects and first-pass metabolism for the query are unknown."
  ],
  "decision_summary": "Predicted high (F ≥ 20%) because all transferable gold-context records for acidic, ionizable, low-permeability-liability molecules report absolute oral bioavailability at or above 20% (azosemide ≈ 20.4%, mosliciguat 23.1%, 6-MNA 35–38%, bumetanide 66–95%), and no affirmative threshold-relevant evidence supports F < 20%. The physicochemical low prior is an uncertain prior, not endpoint proof, and is outweighed by the directionally consistent transferable evidence; confidence is moderate because no direct query measurement exists and azosemide is close to the threshold."
}

